        "problem": "Given that $P(A)=\\frac{1}{2},$ $P(B)=\\frac{2}{5},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{7}{10},$ find $P(A\\cup {B}^{′}).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{7}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the corresponding Venn diagram and shade the region $A\\cup {B}^{′}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064054/assets/5ecc7dae3e6b6e57ccd4a7b8907cb4dd20b0ece400a5768b747f3cb3da788e4b.png)\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup {B}^{′})=\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$",
